alyst_adjustment": -0.11, "key_factors": ["R+9 district with incumbent McDowell out-raising Jefferson roughly six to one", "All ten expert ratings sit at Likely R or stronger, mostly Safe/Solid R", "Prediction markets price Jefferson's win chance near 7%", "No public polling; a Democratic-leaning midterm environment is the main unknown"], "rationale": "The district's nine-point Republican lean, McDowell's dominant fundraising edge, and a unanimous expert consensus of Safe or Solid Republican outweigh a midterm climate that favors Democrats, and markets agree Jefferson is a long shot.", "watch": "Fresh polling showing Jefferson within single digits, or signs the Democratic midterm wave is running stronger than fundamentals suggest, would be the main signals to watch.", "confidence": "medium", "notes": ["No usable general-election polls; forecast rests on fundamentals and expert ratings.", "Incumbent Addison McDowell (R) is running."]}, "overview": "North Carolina's 6th District leans Republican by about nine points, and first-term incumbent Addison McDowell is seeking re-election with a large financial edge. He has raised roughly $1.2 million and holds about $382,000 in cash, while Democrat Cyril Jefferson has raised about $200,000 with under $15,000 on hand. No public general-election polls are available, so the picture rests on district fundamentals, fundraising, and outside assessments.\n\nRaters across the spectrum call the race Safe or Solid Republican, with a few at Likely Republican, and prediction markets give Jefferson roughly a seven percent chance. McDowell has spent the fall highlighting legislative work, including bipartisan committee votes, while Jefferson has focused on economic messaging. The district's lean and the resource gap make McDowell a clear favorite, though a strong Democratic midterm environment is the main factor that could narrow the margin.", "history": [{"date": "2026-09-22", "p_dem": 0.0525, "margin": -10.38}, {"date": "2026-09-23", "p